00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
 Hello and welcome back.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:06,920
 Today we are going to look at a business logic vulnerability that cannot be detected by

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,040
 a traditional security scanners.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:14,200
 The vulnerability is called Confused Deputy and it falls under the always top 10 category

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,360
 of a broken authorization.

6
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:23,280
 In my opinion any company offers remote MCP server might have confused debutability if

7
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:29,040
 they give their MCP server a broad access to a back-end services.

8
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:34,120
 Before we dive into details and the lab, I want to share with you a real-world incident

9
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:41,520
 that happened in remote MCP with a broken authorization happened to Asana in 2025.

10
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:45,280
 I want to read a couple of lines from Sans in Newsletter.

11
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:50,560
 Asana has fixed a vulnerability in their implementation of model context protocol artificial

12
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:56,960
 intelligence integration protocol that could have been exploited to see data belonging

13
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:59,400
 to other organizations.

14
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:04,760
 While there is no technical write-up published about this incident, we can read from those

15
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:10,480
 lines or we can get from those lines that the root cause was a broken authorization in

16
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,600
 the remote MCP server.

17
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:19,440
 In multi-tenant services allowing user A to access data of user B in another organizations,

18
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:20,440
 this is catastrophic.

19
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:25,680
 Let's try to understand why could this happen in the first place.

20
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:31,560
 So when the developers want to introduce an integrate remote MCP server with their back-end

21
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:37,720
 services, they already have API's any place to access their services by customers.

22
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:42,840
 Let's assume those API's have a proper authorization checks, something like validating

23
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:47,840
 authorization token, the token scope, enforcing access control etc.

24
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:53,800
 So everything could be secure from the API perspective that is served by the back-end.

25
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:59,560
 However, now they want to introduce AI capabilities to their platform to better serve their

26
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:02,440
 customers and become more competitive.

27
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:08,160
 The MCP server in this case is going to be the bridge between the customers AI model and

28
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,400
 the data that lives in the back-end services.

29
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:12,680
 So here's the problem.

30
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:18,280
 The MCP server will accept requests from all customers and all users.

31
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:23,520
 And somehow it needs a way to access the data on behalf of those users.

32
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,920
 In order to process the requests.

33
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:32,480
 So how can the MCP server ensure that user A can only access data belonging to a user A

34
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:35,200
 and not that I belong to a user B?

35
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:41,120
 So to solve this problem, developers usually take one of those two approaches.

36
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:46,800
 Option one, they gave the MCP server itself an access token with wide permissions and

37
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:53,880
 the MCP server can use it's own access token to access any data using the existing public

38
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:58,240
 API endpoints provided by the back-end services.

39
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:05,200
 Option B, they could build a dedicated internal endpoint that the MCP server can use again

40
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:07,800
 to access any user's data.

41
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:13,560
 Both approaches are fundamentally flawed because they completely ignore the risk of someone

42
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:19,200
 abusing the MCP server itself to access data of other users.

43
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:26,360
 In other words, if user A asks the MCP server to get data of user B, then the MCP server

44
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:30,560
 can technically do that because it has a broad access.

45
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:35,800
 In my opinion, the secure implementation would be instead of rebuilding the authorization,

46
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:42,160
 verification logic in the MCP server or giving the MCP server broad access with this

47
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,800
 wide permission access token.

48
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,760
 Developers should build the MCP to require the user to pass its own access token to the

49
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:57,520
 MCP first and then the MCP would use the user's access token to the back-end API instead

50
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:03,920
 of using a wide permission access token created for the MCP server itself.

51
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:08,360
 And by doing so, you could reuse the authorization verification that you already have

52
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,440
 at the back-end services level.

53
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:14,520
 In the next lecture, we will see this vulnerability in action, so I hope that this lecture

54
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:17,600
 has been informative for you and I will see you in the next one.

55
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:18,600
 Thanks for watching.

